1
00:00:03,610 --> 00:00:05,130
Item 28:

2
00:00:05,130 --> 00:00:07,280
your pet name for me.

3
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:10,020
Time's running out on this. You need to make a decision.

4
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:12,590
I submitted you a notarized list.

5
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,730
I'm sorry, but "Gollum" and "Flakey" are not acceptable.

6
00:00:16,730 --> 00:00:19,070
Well, you don't like "Princess Corncob"

7
00:00:19,070 --> 00:00:22,310
you don't like "Fester"-- you're just impossible to please.

8
00:00:22,310 --> 00:00:24,220
We'll come back to that one.

9
00:00:24,220 --> 00:00:25,590
This brings us to the final item

10
00:00:25,590 --> 00:00:27,980
in our annual State of the Relationship Summit.

11
00:00:27,980 --> 00:00:31,170
Item 29: Valentine's Day.

12
00:00:31,170 --> 00:00:33,880
Ah, the worst for last.

13
00:00:35,060 --> 00:00:37,180
Classic Flakey.

14
00:00:38,050 --> 00:00:40,580
Before you get upset, I believe I've come up with

15
00:00:40,580 --> 00:00:41,980
a way for us to celebrate the occasion

16
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:43,480
that we both can enjoy.

17
00:00:43,670 --> 00:00:45,890
People usually start a meeting with a joke,

18
00:00:45,890 --> 00:00:48,440
but you go ahead-- end with one.

19
00:00:49,330 --> 00:00:51,350
I propose we spend a weekend

20
00:00:51,350 --> 00:00:53,650
at a bed-and-breakfast in Napa Valley.

21
00:00:53,650 --> 00:00:56,880
I hate every word in that sentence.

22
00:00:57,440 --> 00:01:01,090
Including "in" "at," "we" and "a".

23
00:01:01,660 --> 00:01:03,310
Come on, Sheldon. Why not?

24
00:01:03,310 --> 00:01:05,950
For starters, a bed-and-breakfast forces you

25
00:01:05,950 --> 00:01:08,040
to eat with strangers at your table.

26
00:01:08,040 --> 00:01:09,080
One step ahead of you--

27
00:01:09,080 --> 00:01:11,120
Bernadette and Wolowitz are going with us.

28
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:12,040
Oh, very well.

29
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:13,630
What are the sleeping arrangements?

30
00:01:13,630 --> 00:01:16,320
We've only been dating for three years.

31
00:01:16,320 --> 00:01:19,590
If we were to share a room, people might talk.

32
00:01:20,170 --> 00:01:21,640
I got you your own room.

33
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:24,060
- What if my room has a claw-foot bathtub? - It doesn't.

34
00:01:24,060 --> 00:01:27,060
I know it makes you feel like you're bathing inside a monster.

35
00:01:28,330 --> 00:01:30,180
Look, I appreciate the effort,

36
00:01:30,180 --> 00:01:32,150
but I'm still unclear how this trip

37
00:01:32,150 --> 00:01:35,020
is supposed to be enjoyable for me.

38
00:01:35,020 --> 00:01:37,210
We're going to have Valentine's Day dinner

39
00:01:37,210 --> 00:01:40,830
on a fully functioning vintage train.

40
00:01:41,310 --> 00:01:43,850
Vintage? Be specific.

41
00:01:43,850 --> 00:01:46,320
An Alcoa FA-4 diesel locomotive

42
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:48,450
leading a train of meticulously restored

43
00:01:48,450 --> 00:01:51,760
1915 Pullman first-class coaches.

44
00:01:54,780 --> 00:01:57,440
I'm feeling the urge to hug you.

45
00:01:57,440 --> 00:02:00,520
And one... and two...

46
00:02:00,520 --> 00:02:02,620
All right, Gollum, we're good.

47
00:02:29,480 --> 00:02:31,290
Are you sure you guys don't want to come with us to Napa?

48
00:02:31,310 --> 00:02:32,700
You could probably still get a room.

49
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:35,280
No, I think we're just gonna have a quiet weekend at home.

50
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:36,780
Plus, I'm not sure it's a great idea

51
00:02:36,780 --> 00:02:39,490
to take Penny to where wine comes from.

52
00:02:41,910 --> 00:02:43,340
What? It's a joke.

53
00:02:43,340 --> 00:02:45,650
Oh, come on. We bust on each other.

54
00:02:45,650 --> 00:02:48,020
I wear dorky glasses, you might have a problem--

55
00:02:48,020 --> 00:02:49,760
it's all for laughs.

56
00:02:50,870 --> 00:02:54,040
That would really piss me off if I didn't have a buzz going on.

57
00:02:57,820 --> 00:03:00,670
Howard, which pocket watch will you be wearing

58
00:03:00,670 --> 00:03:02,880
for dinner on the train?

59
00:03:07,940 --> 00:03:09,760
I'm sorry, what?

60
00:03:10,150 --> 00:03:12,120
Oh, I'm afraid if we wear the same pocket watch,

61
00:03:12,120 --> 00:03:14,610
it will be embarrassing.

62
00:03:15,780 --> 00:03:20,920
- I don't own a pocket watch. - Oh, my.

63
00:03:20,920 --> 00:03:25,150
Well, then my apologies for bringing up this sore spot.

64
00:03:28,060 --> 00:03:30,200
Since you two are gonna be around for Valentine's,

65
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:31,570
would you mind watching Cinnamon?

66
00:03:31,570 --> 00:03:33,490
You have Valentine's plans?!

67
00:03:33,490 --> 00:03:36,840
Which came out sounding way more surprised than I meant.

68
00:03:37,650 --> 00:03:39,060
L-Let me try that again. You have Val...

69
00:03:39,060 --> 00:03:41,050
See? I can't do it.

70
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:44,630
I don't have plans, which is why I booked time

71
00:03:44,630 --> 00:03:46,410
on the big telescope that night.

72
00:03:46,410 --> 00:03:48,160
Well, an evening looking at the stars--

73
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:49,430
that's still kind of romantic.

74
00:03:49,430 --> 00:03:50,600
Except I'll be alone.

75
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:53,930
I'm trying to put lipstick on a pig here. Work with me.

76
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:57,140
- We'd be happy to watch Cinnamon. - Yeah.

77
00:03:57,140 --> 00:03:57,790
Thank you.

78
00:03:57,790 --> 00:04:00,060
Oh, and I'd like for at least one of us to see some action,

79
00:04:00,060 --> 00:04:01,390
so if you guys happen to have sex,

80
00:04:01,390 --> 00:04:03,690
it's cool if she stays in the room.

81
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:07,450
Hey, same goes for the two of you with Amy.

82
00:04:19,750 --> 00:04:21,590
What do you think, Sheldon?

83
00:04:21,590 --> 00:04:23,650
It's magnificent.

84
00:04:23,650 --> 00:04:26,400
This is going to be the best Valentine's Day ever.

85
00:04:26,400 --> 00:04:28,000
I'm so glad you like it.

86
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:29,900
I'm prepared to say I love it,

87
00:04:29,900 --> 00:04:33,340
as soon as I confirm there are no hobos aboard.

88
00:04:36,400 --> 00:04:37,730
Okay.

89
00:04:39,230 --> 00:04:42,320
Raj, I got it. Bye.

90
00:04:44,350 --> 00:04:46,320
That was your daddy.

91
00:04:47,350 --> 00:04:48,880
He wanted me to say that he misses...

92
00:04:48,880 --> 00:04:51,240
Why am I doing this?

93
00:04:52,250 --> 00:04:54,500
Happy Valentine's Day!

94
00:04:54,500 --> 00:04:56,690
Ooh, flowers and chocolates?

95
00:04:56,690 --> 00:04:59,040
Somebody's trying to get me out of my panties.

96
00:05:00,270 --> 00:05:02,420
Don't be surprised if you find five chocolates missing

97
00:05:02,420 --> 00:05:05,660
and three gross coconut ones with a bite taken out.

98
00:05:06,540 --> 00:05:08,870
It came that way when I bought it.

99
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:11,210
Got you a little something, too.

100
00:05:11,800 --> 00:05:13,820
Jewelry.

101
00:05:13,870 --> 00:05:15,580
Oh, my God.

102
00:05:15,580 --> 00:05:17,750
Lakers tickets?!

103
00:05:19,770 --> 00:05:20,970
It gets better. Instead of me,

104
00:05:20,970 --> 00:05:23,010
you can take someone who will actually enjoy it.

105
00:05:24,360 --> 00:05:28,400
- You are the best boyfriend ever. - Thank you.

106
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:30,850
Seriously, please don't make me go.

107
00:05:33,420 --> 00:05:35,180
Should we take this little party to the bedroom?

108
00:05:35,180 --> 00:05:37,350
Don't have to-- we have the whole place to ourselves.

109
00:05:37,350 --> 00:05:39,720
- Oh, that's true. - In fact, if you want,

110
00:05:39,720 --> 00:05:42,870
we can do it right here on Sheldon's spot.

111
00:05:44,740 --> 00:05:48,460
That is the least sexy thing anyone's ever said to me.

112
00:05:49,050 --> 00:05:50,480
To the bedroom!

113
00:05:56,190 --> 00:05:59,610
And for the entree, tonight's special is a seafood risotto.

114
00:05:59,610 --> 00:06:00,810
Do you have any questions?

115
00:06:00,810 --> 00:06:02,420
Uh, I do.

116
00:06:02,420 --> 00:06:05,820
Uh, does this train car have the original link-and-pin coupler

117
00:06:05,820 --> 00:06:08,150
or the Miller Hook and Platform?

118
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:11,440
I'm sorry, I meant questions about the food.

119
00:06:11,440 --> 00:06:13,130
Oh, of course. Um...

120
00:06:13,130 --> 00:06:16,170
is the seafood risotto being served on a train car

121
00:06:16,170 --> 00:06:17,970
with the original link-and-pin coupler

122
00:06:17,970 --> 00:06:20,450
or the Miller Hook and Platform?

123
00:06:21,570 --> 00:06:23,970
I-I think we're gonna need a minute.

124
00:06:24,970 --> 00:06:28,470
Fun fact: it's neither.

125
00:06:29,480 --> 00:06:32,600
They actually use the AAR type E coupler.

126
00:06:32,600 --> 00:06:35,270
If you listen carefully when the locomotive disconnects,

127
00:06:35,270 --> 00:06:36,460
you'll hear the characteristic

128
00:06:36,460 --> 00:06:40,350
click-pshht-thunk of the knuckle.

129
00:06:42,600 --> 00:06:45,060
Get out of town.

130
00:06:45,870 --> 00:06:49,840
Fun fact: I'm gonna jump off this train.

131
00:06:53,660 --> 00:06:55,060
Hey, Cinnamon,

132
00:06:55,070 --> 00:06:58,020
guess who just did it human style.

133
00:06:59,110 --> 00:07:00,870
Oh, my God!

134
00:07:00,870 --> 00:07:02,980
It's a little late, but I'll take it.

135
00:07:03,790 --> 00:07:05,710
No, Cinnamon ate the chocolates.

136
00:07:05,710 --> 00:07:07,500
That's really bad for dogs.

137
00:07:07,500 --> 00:07:09,370
Oh, crap. What are we gonna do?

138
00:07:09,370 --> 00:07:11,040
We gotta get her to a vet right now.

139
00:07:11,040 --> 00:07:12,360
I don't have a vet.

140
00:07:12,360 --> 00:07:15,040
I have a podiatrist, an optometrist, an allergist,

141
00:07:15,040 --> 00:07:16,560
a dermatologist, a urologist.

142
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:18,750
You'd think I'd have a vet.

143
00:07:18,750 --> 00:07:20,930
Okay. There's one not far from here. Come on. Let's go.

144
00:07:20,930 --> 00:07:22,430
Okay. Koothrappali was right.

145
00:07:22,430 --> 00:07:24,940
We should have let her watch.

146
00:07:27,920 --> 00:07:29,730
Do another one, do another one!

147
00:07:30,330 --> 00:07:31,110
Okay.

148
00:07:31,110 --> 00:07:33,310
Here's my impression of the Amtrak Acela

149
00:07:33,310 --> 00:07:35,870
barreling down the Eastern Corridor.

150
00:07:43,640 --> 00:07:46,810
It's like there's a train in your mouth.

151
00:07:47,830 --> 00:07:48,780
Oh, yeah. I've got one.

152
00:07:48,780 --> 00:07:53,260
Um, the Amtrak Wolverine coming into Chicago.

153
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:03,450
I've been on that train.

154
00:08:03,450 --> 00:08:05,850
And I just was again.

155
00:08:07,310 --> 00:08:09,120
Hey, see if you guys can guess this one.

156
00:08:09,120 --> 00:08:11,900
Bang! Splat! Thud.

157
00:08:13,880 --> 00:08:16,650
- How many trains have you been on? - Tons.

158
00:08:16,650 --> 00:08:19,540
A box fell on my head at UPS six years ago.

159
00:08:19,540 --> 00:08:22,890
Now I just collect disability and ride trains.

160
00:08:25,320 --> 00:08:27,890
Your life's amazing.

161
00:08:28,110 --> 00:08:29,120
Not always.

162
00:08:29,120 --> 00:08:31,920
A box fell on my head at UPS six years ago.

163
00:08:31,920 --> 00:08:35,620
Now I just collect disability and ride trains.

164
00:08:40,240 --> 00:08:42,090
Why do I even try?

165
00:08:42,090 --> 00:08:44,060
- I'm gonna fix this right now. - Okay.

166
00:08:44,060 --> 00:08:46,550
Just make it look like an accident.

167
00:08:49,420 --> 00:08:50,510
Excuse me.

168
00:08:50,510 --> 00:08:53,040
You are at Valentine's dinner with your girlfriend.

169
00:08:53,040 --> 00:08:55,360
Now get back over there and be with her.

170
00:08:56,910 --> 00:08:57,840
You're right.

171
00:08:57,840 --> 00:08:59,910
That was insensitive of me.

172
00:09:00,670 --> 00:09:02,400
I have to go back to my table now.

173
00:09:02,400 --> 00:09:04,620
- You should join us. - All right.

174
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:08,430
Great.

175
00:09:08,430 --> 00:09:10,960
Now there's two of 'em.

176
00:09:16,690 --> 00:09:18,510
How much chocolate did she eat?

177
00:09:18,510 --> 00:09:20,280
- A whole box. - Well, to be fair,

178
00:09:20,280 --> 00:09:22,760
you ate a lot of it before you gave it to me.

179
00:09:23,990 --> 00:09:26,860
So the point is I may have saved her life.

180
00:09:27,790 --> 00:09:30,130
I'm sorry, is this a joke to you?

181
00:09:30,130 --> 00:09:31,690
No.

182
00:09:31,690 --> 00:09:33,870
Maybe to her.

183
00:09:35,900 --> 00:09:37,700
How big a box of chocolate was it?

184
00:09:37,700 --> 00:09:39,300
Uh, something like this. I don't know.

185
00:09:39,300 --> 00:09:42,130
It came free with a full tank of gas.

186
00:09:42,590 --> 00:09:43,380
Really?

187
00:09:43,380 --> 00:09:44,970
Do you know how much those Lakers tickets were?

188
00:09:44,970 --> 00:09:46,130
Do you know how much gas is?

189
00:09:47,450 --> 00:09:49,610
- Sorry. - Sorry.

190
00:09:50,540 --> 00:09:53,110
Oh, my God, Cinnamon, are you okay?!

191
00:09:53,110 --> 00:09:54,870
I can't believe you two!

192
00:09:54,870 --> 00:09:56,360
You do whatever it takes to save her life.

193
00:09:56,360 --> 00:09:58,440
If she needs new organs, I'll buy any dog necessary

194
00:09:58,440 --> 00:10:00,910
and scrap them for parts!

195
00:10:01,840 --> 00:10:02,920
You're the owner?

196
00:10:02,920 --> 00:10:04,910
Owner, father, soul mate,

197
00:10:04,910 --> 00:10:09,550
and if anything happens to her, your worst nightmare!

198
00:10:10,680 --> 00:10:11,980
Well, she's not throwing up,

199
00:10:11,980 --> 00:10:13,090
which is a good sign.

200
00:10:13,090 --> 00:10:15,490
So, I'm gonna take her in back, put her on fluids

201
00:10:15,490 --> 00:10:17,260
and give her something to absorb the toxins.

202
00:10:17,260 --> 00:10:19,090
- Okay? - Okay, thank you.

203
00:10:19,090 --> 00:10:20,680
Oh, if she's scared, you can sing to her.

204
00:10:20,680 --> 00:10:23,370
She likes Katy Perry.

205
00:10:23,370 --> 00:10:24,470
Oh, but don't do "Firework"

206
00:10:24,470 --> 00:10:26,330
That gets her all riled up.

207
00:10:26,740 --> 00:10:28,040
Got it.

208
00:10:28,590 --> 00:10:30,490
Should have been a dentist.

209
00:10:32,290 --> 00:10:34,650
Okay, what was the best four-ten-four

210
00:10:34,650 --> 00:10:36,510
U.S. Locomotive ever built?

211
00:10:36,510 --> 00:10:38,940
Trick question. There never was one.

212
00:10:38,940 --> 00:10:40,050
Or was there?

213
00:10:40,320 --> 00:10:42,250
What?!

214
00:10:43,870 --> 00:10:47,450
In 1944, the Pacific Railroad built number 6131,

215
00:10:47,450 --> 00:10:49,360
which rearranged the drivers and cylinders,

216
00:10:49,360 --> 00:10:50,910
solving the problem of the Q-1,

217
00:10:50,910 --> 00:10:53,100
creating a duplex-drive four-four-six-four.

218
00:10:53,100 --> 00:10:54,420
In what world is

219
00:10:54,420 --> 00:10:56,750
a four-four-six-four a four-ten-four?

220
00:10:56,750 --> 00:10:58,770
A world I don't want to live in!

221
00:11:00,490 --> 00:11:03,630
Seriously, I no longer want to live in this world.

222
00:11:04,040 --> 00:11:05,500
Hold on to your conductor's hat.

223
00:11:05,500 --> 00:11:07,390
You crank the second and third axles,

224
00:11:07,390 --> 00:11:09,790
creating a pair of internal connecting rods,

225
00:11:09,790 --> 00:11:12,020
and boom-- four-ten-four.

226
00:11:12,020 --> 00:11:13,430
If you think about it, the Q-2

227
00:11:13,430 --> 00:11:15,580
was like the four-ten-four America never made.

228
00:11:15,580 --> 00:11:18,120
I may never stop thinking about it.

229
00:11:18,120 --> 00:11:20,710
Amy, what are the odds we run into this guy?

230
00:11:20,710 --> 00:11:23,070
Better than you think.

231
00:11:25,520 --> 00:11:26,780
You know, if you ask nicely,

232
00:11:26,780 --> 00:11:28,810
they'll let you visit the engine room.

233
00:11:29,450 --> 00:11:31,760
I never want this day to end.

234
00:11:31,760 --> 00:11:34,590
It's feeling like it never will.

235
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:36,080
Come on.

236
00:11:37,110 --> 00:11:40,100
Hey, did I tell you what happened to me at UPS?

237
00:11:42,930 --> 00:11:45,110
As if Valentine's Day wasn't bad enough,

238
00:11:45,110 --> 00:11:46,670
you try to kill my dog?!

239
00:11:46,670 --> 00:11:48,830
And with cheap chocolate, no less?

240
00:11:48,830 --> 00:11:50,400
It wasn't cheap.

241
00:11:50,400 --> 00:11:52,440
It was free.

242
00:11:53,150 --> 00:11:54,960
We're really sorry. It was an accident.

243
00:11:54,960 --> 00:11:56,740
Yeah, we weren't even out of the room that long.

244
00:11:56,740 --> 00:11:59,650
Oh, no, come on. It was a while.

245
00:12:00,840 --> 00:12:02,430
It was a while.

246
00:12:04,710 --> 00:12:06,350
Oh, is Cinnamon gonna be okay?

247
00:12:06,350 --> 00:12:07,510
She's responding well.

248
00:12:07,510 --> 00:12:10,150
We just want to keep her a little longer for observation.

249
00:12:10,150 --> 00:12:12,550
All right. Uh, would-would it be okay for me to see her?

250
00:12:12,550 --> 00:12:13,920
We're usually in bed by now,

251
00:12:13,920 --> 00:12:16,170
and I want her to know that I'm here.

252
00:12:16,900 --> 00:12:20,110
Yes, we sleep together, and sometimes we spoon.

253
00:12:22,160 --> 00:12:24,580
It's okay. I sleep with my dog, too.

254
00:12:25,130 --> 00:12:26,510
We're not supposed to let people in back,

255
00:12:26,510 --> 00:12:29,150
but I think I can make an exception.

256
00:12:29,150 --> 00:12:31,180
- Thank you. - Come on.

257
00:12:31,870 --> 00:12:33,780
By the way, I sang her Katy Perry.

258
00:12:33,780 --> 00:12:35,310
- Oh, yeah? - And I don't care

259
00:12:35,310 --> 00:12:36,930
what that obnoxious parrot back there says.

260
00:12:36,930 --> 00:12:38,800
I crushed it.

261
00:12:40,240 --> 00:12:42,400
I think there's something going on between the two of them.

262
00:12:42,400 --> 00:12:45,050
Maybe, but you also think nine minutes isn't a while,

263
00:12:45,050 --> 00:12:46,910
so what do you know?

264
00:12:53,870 --> 00:12:56,180
- You okay? - Why?

265
00:12:56,180 --> 00:12:59,740
Because my boyfriend's off playing choo-choo with some weirdo?

266
00:13:00,490 --> 00:13:04,540
Well, to be fair, they're both weirdos.

267
00:13:05,050 --> 00:13:07,540
I don't know what made me think tonight would be any different.

268
00:13:07,540 --> 00:13:10,700
Well, just the fact that you got him up here still says a lot.

269
00:13:10,760 --> 00:13:12,320
To be honest, I bet Howie 200 bucks

270
00:13:12,320 --> 00:13:13,520
it wasn't gonna happen.

271
00:13:13,520 --> 00:13:17,410
I'm going to the Lego store to get a big-ass R2-D2.

272
00:13:20,330 --> 00:13:21,750
See? It's not just Sheldon.

273
00:13:21,750 --> 00:13:23,470
They're all idiots.

274
00:13:25,050 --> 00:13:26,440
She's right.

275
00:13:27,560 --> 00:13:29,090
So, your boyfriend's a fixer-upper.

276
00:13:29,090 --> 00:13:30,330
Most of them are.

277
00:13:30,330 --> 00:13:31,260
I mean, look at this guy.

278
00:13:31,260 --> 00:13:32,640
You think he came like this?

279
00:13:32,640 --> 00:13:35,590
When I met him, he was a hot, goofy mess.

280
00:13:36,750 --> 00:13:38,820
Now, he's been to space.

281
00:13:38,820 --> 00:13:41,020
That's all me.

282
00:13:42,950 --> 00:13:44,930
I had a little to do with it.

283
00:13:44,930 --> 00:13:46,290
Oh, sure you did.

284
00:13:46,290 --> 00:13:49,400
Who's Mama's big space man?

285
00:13:50,710 --> 00:13:52,840
I am.

286
00:13:59,250 --> 00:14:00,870
They've been in there, like, half an hour.

287
00:14:00,870 --> 00:14:04,050
Yeah, for future reference, that's a while.

288
00:14:08,370 --> 00:14:10,190
- How long should we stay? - I don't know.

289
00:14:10,190 --> 00:14:11,570
I'm kind of hungry.

290
00:14:11,570 --> 00:14:13,560
I saw a Thai place next door.

291
00:14:13,560 --> 00:14:15,370
Oh. Okay.

292
00:14:16,540 --> 00:14:18,070
- Hey! - How's she doing?

293
00:14:18,070 --> 00:14:19,840
- How is everything? - Yeah. She's gonna be okay;

294
00:14:19,840 --> 00:14:22,040
- they're gonna let me take her home. - Oh, good.

295
00:14:22,040 --> 00:14:23,500
So I want you to keep a close eye on her

296
00:14:23,500 --> 00:14:25,310
for the next 24 hours.

297
00:14:25,310 --> 00:14:27,590
Here are a few warning signs to look out for,

298
00:14:27,590 --> 00:14:30,570
and if you see any of them, don't hesitate to call.

299
00:14:30,570 --> 00:14:33,360
Thank you for taking such good care of my little girl.

300
00:14:33,360 --> 00:14:35,570
My pleasure. Have a nice night.

301
00:14:35,570 --> 00:14:37,530
- Yes, thank you for all your help. - Yeah.

302
00:14:37,530 --> 00:14:39,230
I'm sorry if you didn't think we were taking it seriously.

303
00:14:39,230 --> 00:14:40,820
We love animals.

304
00:14:40,820 --> 00:14:42,400
Oh, do you guys own any pets?

305
00:14:42,400 --> 00:14:45,160
- No. - Good.

306
00:14:51,740 --> 00:14:53,580
I love you so much.

307
00:14:53,580 --> 00:14:55,630
I love you, too.

308
00:15:03,460 --> 00:15:05,750
- Sorry. - Sorry.

309
00:15:06,290 --> 00:15:08,120
You guys missed a pretty great time.

310
00:15:08,120 --> 00:15:11,840
The conductor and I have the same pocket watch.

311
00:15:13,250 --> 00:15:14,590
It was crazy.

312
00:15:14,590 --> 00:15:17,290
Dare I say "loco"?

313
00:15:17,860 --> 00:15:19,040
Oh, and, Amy, guess what?

314
00:15:19,040 --> 00:15:21,210
The conductor said as soon as he gets off work,

315
00:15:21,210 --> 00:15:22,590
he can come back to the bed and breakfast

316
00:15:22,590 --> 00:15:25,220
and play his banjo for us.

317
00:15:25,970 --> 00:15:28,450
Okay. I need to speak to my boyfriend in private,

318
00:15:28,450 --> 00:15:29,920
like, right now.

319
00:15:29,920 --> 00:15:31,420
There's a car with a glass roof.

320
00:15:31,420 --> 00:15:32,750
Want to go look at the stars?

321
00:15:32,750 --> 00:15:34,340
Oh, that sounds so romantic.

322
00:15:34,340 --> 00:15:36,460
Oh, give it a rest!

323
00:15:37,350 --> 00:15:38,850
Let's go.

324
00:15:42,910 --> 00:15:44,480
Why are you still here?

325
00:15:44,480 --> 00:15:45,850
Excuse me.

326
00:15:45,850 --> 00:15:47,640
I think you're being a little rude.

327
00:15:47,640 --> 00:15:48,630
I'm being rude?

328
00:15:48,630 --> 00:15:50,580
You've been rude to me this entire evening.

329
00:15:50,580 --> 00:15:52,230
How is that possible?

330
00:15:52,230 --> 00:15:55,660
I've hardly spoken to you since we got on the train.

331
00:15:57,080 --> 00:15:59,630
I'm detecting a little friction between you two,

332
00:15:59,630 --> 00:16:02,550
and I don't want to be a third rail.

333
00:16:02,550 --> 00:16:04,170
Get it?

334
00:16:04,810 --> 00:16:07,080
I get it.

335
00:16:08,250 --> 00:16:09,610
Leave!

336
00:16:11,670 --> 00:16:13,110
What is your problem?

337
00:16:13,110 --> 00:16:14,230
It's Valentine's Day.

338
00:16:14,230 --> 00:16:16,630
We're supposed to be having a romantic weekend.

339
00:16:16,630 --> 00:16:18,850
Oh, really? Because I remember you saying

340
00:16:18,850 --> 00:16:21,730
that this trip was going to be something we could both enjoy.

341
00:16:21,730 --> 00:16:22,980
Did you mean that,

342
00:16:22,980 --> 00:16:25,140
or were you just trying to trick me?

343
00:16:25,140 --> 00:16:26,980
Fine, it's true.

344
00:16:26,980 --> 00:16:28,990
I deserve romance,

345
00:16:28,990 --> 00:16:32,150
and I didn't know how else to make it happen.

346
00:16:32,510 --> 00:16:36,510
Well, if you want romance, then let's have romance!

347
00:16:36,510 --> 00:16:38,550
Oh, look, there's wine.

348
00:16:40,870 --> 00:16:43,320
Grape juice that burns.

349
00:16:43,720 --> 00:16:46,200
Uh, now let's gaze into each other's eyes, hmm?

350
00:16:46,200 --> 00:16:47,990
You blinked. I win.

351
00:16:47,990 --> 00:16:49,170
- Sheldon... - Let's see. What's next?

352
00:16:49,170 --> 00:16:50,920
Oh, kissing's romantic.

353
00:17:09,340 --> 00:17:11,750
That was nice.

354
00:17:12,370 --> 00:17:14,090
Good.

355
00:17:18,590 --> 00:17:22,230
The conductor said if I come back to the engine room,

356
00:17:22,230 --> 00:17:26,420
he'd show me how to bring the train through a crossing.

357
00:17:27,500 --> 00:17:30,090
Okay, have fun.

358
00:17:31,790 --> 00:17:34,040
Do you want to come with me?

359
00:17:34,040 --> 00:17:37,490
Really? I do.

360
00:17:40,530 --> 00:17:42,350
Hey, guys, wait up!

361
00:17:49,220 --> 00:17:51,130
There you go.

362
00:17:51,130 --> 00:17:54,050
All cozy wozy.

363
00:17:54,630 --> 00:18:00,080
Here, let's see what the doctor says to keep an eye out for.

364
00:18:00,790 --> 00:18:03,440
"Rajesh, I was dreading Valentine's Day.

365
00:18:03,440 --> 00:18:07,900
Thank you for spending it with me. Yvette."

366
00:18:08,840 --> 00:18:12,300
Cinnamon, she-she gave me her phone number.

367
00:18:12,730 --> 00:18:13,580
If I knew it was that easy,

368
00:18:13,580 --> 00:18:16,690
I would have considered poisoning you months ago.

369
00:18:18,900 --> 00:18:20,430
Oh, what should I say?

370
00:18:20,430 --> 00:18:23,100
Oh, I know. I'll point out her name's Yvette,

371
00:18:23,100 --> 00:18:24,790
and that she's a vet.

372
00:18:24,790 --> 00:18:26,450
That's hysterical.

373
00:18:26,450 --> 00:18:28,050
She'll love it.

374
00:18:31,030 --> 00:18:32,480
- Hello. - You're back.

375
00:18:32,480 --> 00:18:35,010
- How was your trip? It was wonderful!

376
00:18:35,010 --> 00:18:36,160
Great. What did you do?

377
00:18:36,160 --> 00:18:37,740
I made a new friend

378
00:18:37,740 --> 00:18:39,660
who likes trains as much as I do,

379
00:18:39,660 --> 00:18:41,530
uh, I kissed Amy on the lips,

380
00:18:41,530 --> 00:18:44,330
and, uh, the conductor played his banjo for me.

381
00:18:44,330 --> 00:18:45,800
Good night.

382
00:18:46,500 --> 00:18:47,300
Wait, wait, wait, wait, wait, wait.

383
00:18:47,300 --> 00:18:49,620
I'm gonna need more details.

384
00:18:49,620 --> 00:18:52,230
Oh, well, my new friend's name was Eric.

385
00:18:52,230 --> 00:18:55,780
Um, Amy lips tasted like the brownie we had for dessert.

386
00:18:56,700 --> 00:18:58,150
the banjo-playing conductor

387
00:18:58,150 --> 00:18:59,340
was missing a finger,

388
00:18:59,340 --> 00:19:02,450
but he made up for it with his can-do attitude.

389
00:19:02,450 --> 00:19:04,050
No, hang on. Hang on.

390
00:19:04,220 --> 00:19:07,360
Are all those things equal to you?

391
00:19:08,440 --> 00:19:11,450
It never occurred to me to pick a favorite.

392
00:19:11,450 --> 00:19:13,810
Well, give it a go.

393
00:19:14,730 --> 00:19:18,330
I can't answer that without collecting additional data.

394
00:19:19,040 --> 00:19:21,970
Additional data. You dog!

395
00:19:23,250 --> 00:19:25,590
I'm not sure how listening to other nine-fingered banjo players

396
00:19:25,590 --> 00:19:28,000
makes me dog, but all right.

